# TARGET ARCHITECTURE: x86_64 | v0.16.2 | gcp/ubuntu2404
# Enterprise Linux Audit Policy v0.16.2 | gcp/ubuntu2404 | Tier 2 — PROPOSAL; inactive pending collection-volume decision
# Objective: persistent/system security config changes irrespective of CVE association.
# /etc exists on standard Linux hosts; an individual /etc/ld.so.preload file might NOT.
# Directory-based audit catches modifications and creation under /etc, including
# /etc/ld.so.preload, /etc/profile, /etc/profile.d, /etc/ssh and sudoers IF present.
# Not enabled by default: broad /etc collection needs tier-2 promotion approval,
# duplicate/overlap review and volume justification for busy and cloud hosts.
# Audits writes/attribute changes, NOT file reads; covers both ABI variants.
# # -a always,exit -F dir=/etc -F perm=wa -k t2_etc_integrity

# CVE-independent Magic SysRq control; conditional to kernel/sysctl support.
# /etc/sysctl.conf and /etc/sysctl.d changes are covered by the active /etc rule.
# This optional procfs watch must be separately validated before uncommenting:
# -a always,exit -F path=/proc/sysrq-trigger -F perm=w -k t2_sysrq
# -a always,exit -F path=/proc/sys/kernel/sysrq -F perm=wa -k t2_sysrq

# Narrow leaf-path alternative, only where present on the chosen image:
# -a always,exit -F path=/etc/ld.so.preload -F perm=wa -k t2_loader_preload
# Warning: /etc/ld.so.preload is often absent, so this is not reliable for creation.

